## 2. Credential resolution

### 2.1 New file: `groovy/src/main/groovy/ai/worklog/framework/adapters/AutomoxCredentials.groovy`

Responsible for producing a resolved profile and for guaranteeing that no
secret leaves it except through explicit accessor methods.

Resolution precedence per field, highest first:

1. Environment variables: `AUTOMOX_API_TOKEN`, `AUTOMOX_ENROLLMENT_KEY`, `AUTOMOX_ORG`, `AUTOMOX_API_BASE_URL`
2. Profile entry in `integrations/automox/automox.properties`
3. Legacy files in `integrations/automox/`: `token` and `server-id`
4. Shared-rules or workspace-config defaults for non-secret fields only

Profile selection precedence: `--profile` option, then `AUTOMOX_PROFILE`, then
`adapters.automox.default_profile` from workspace config, then
`default_profile` from shared rules.

Properties file format, parsed by the existing `PropertiesSupport.load` and
grouped by the existing `PropertiesSupport.controllers` prefix-splitting
behaviour. Recognised fields per profile: `org`, `api_token`,
`enrollment_key`, `api_base_url`, `domain`, `ssh_user`.

Legacy file parsing rules:

- `token` — take the last non-empty, non-comment line, trimmed
- `server-id` — parse with a line-oriented regular expression for an assignment to `AUTOMOX_SETKEY`, stripping surrounding single or double quotes. The file must never be executed or sourced.

Public methods:

- `resolve(FrameworkPaths paths, Map config, String requestedProfile)` returning a resolved profile object
- `publicProfile(String id, resolved)` returning a map with `id`, `org`, `api_base_url`, `source` (one of `env`, `properties`, `legacy`, `default` per field group), `has_api_token`, `has_enrollment_key` — and no secret values
- `publicProfiles(...)` returning the sorted list of the above
- `authorizationHeaders(resolved)` returning the `Authorization` and `Accept` headers, called only from inside the adapter

The existing `PropertiesSupport.publicController` and `publicControllers` must
not be modified, to avoid disturbing Jenkins behaviour and its Groovy tests.

### 2.2 New file: `groovy/src/main/groovy/ai/worklog/framework/adapters/AutomoxWriteClient.groovy`

Wraps `ReadOnlyHttp.request` for non-GET methods so that read-only semantics of
`ReadOnlyHttp` remain truthful.

- Constructor takes a `ReadOnlyHttp` instance and a boolean `apply`
- A `send(method, url, headers, body, timeoutSeconds)` method that throws `IllegalStateException` when `apply` is false, so a dry-run can never issue a mutating request even through a coding mistake
- No changes to `ReadOnlyHttp.groovy`

## 3. Adapter

### 3.1 New file: `groovy/src/main/groovy/ai/worklog/framework/adapters/AutomoxAdapter.groovy`

Constructor parameters mirroring `JenkinsAdapter`: `FrameworkPaths paths`,
`ReadOnlyHttp http`, `Map overrides`, `File frameworkRoot`, `Map config`, and
additionally a boolean `apply`.

`settings()` returns a map merging, in order, shared-rules defaults then
`config.adapters.automox`: `timeout_seconds`, `api_base_url`, `org`, `domain`,
`default_profile`, `devices_limit`, `groups_limit`, `policies_limit`.

`operatorRules()` reads `shared/automox-operator-rules.json` through
`JsonFiles.read` with an inline default map, following the
`JenkinsAdapter.operatorRules` pattern.

Read operations, each returning a payload map shaped for
`AutomoxOperatorReport`:

| Method | HTTP | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `operatorProfiles()` | none | Lists redacted profiles; BLOCKED when none configured, message `No automox.properties found or no profiles configured` |
| `operatorAuthTest(profile, timeout)` | GET orgs with limit 1 | READY on 200, BLOCKED on 401/403, DEGRADED on other non-200 |
| `operatorOrgs(profile, timeout)` | GET orgs | Items carry `id`, `name`, `device_count` |
| `operatorGroups(profile, query, limit, timeout)` | GET server groups | Client-side case-insensitive substring filter on name; unnamed groups rendered as `(unnamed)` |
| `operatorGroup(profile, groupId, timeout)` | GET server groups then servers by group | Group metadata plus member devices; ERROR with message `Group not found` when absent |
| `operatorDevices(profile, groupId, query, limit, timeout)` | GET servers | Optional `groupId` query parameter; client-side name filter |
| `operatorDevice(profile, reference, withPackages, timeout)` | GET server, optionally packages | Reference resolution per 3.2; packages summarised as pending count, total count, and first `packages_preview` entries |
| `operatorPolicies(profile, limit, timeout)` | GET policies | Items carry `id`, `name`, `policy_type_name`, `schedule_time`, `server_groups`, and `auto_patch`/`auto_reboot` from `configuration` |
| `operatorPolicy(profile, policyId, timeout)` | GET policy | Populates the report `schedule` object per 3.3 |
| `operatorPolicyStats(profile, policyId, timeout)` | GET policy stats | Optional client-side filter by policy id; ERROR `Policy stats not found` when a requested id is absent |

Write operations:

| Method | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `operatorDeviceMove(profile, reference, groupId, timeout)` | Resolves device, reads current `server_group_id`, produces a `change` object with `from`, `to`, `dry_run`, `applied`. When `apply` is false it returns the planned change with status READY and performs no request. When true it issues PUT with body containing only `server_group_id`, validates the response code against `device_move.success_codes`, then re-reads the device to confirm |
| `operatorPolicyAddGroup(profile, policyId, groupId, timeout)` | Reads the policy, computes the target `server_groups` list, and builds the payload from `policy_update.required_fields` only. When `apply` is false it returns the planned group list and the two-step plan with no request. When true it performs the rename round-trip: first PUT with `name` suffixed by `rename_suffix`, then PUT with the original name and updated groups; both codes validated against `policy_update.success_codes`; on failure of the second step the report status is ERROR and the message names the policy as left in the renamed state |

All write payload construction must go through `AutomoxWriteClient`, and no
temporary files may be used; payloads are built and held in memory.

### 3.2 Device reference resolution

A private method taking a reference string:

1. If it matches digits only, treat as a device id
2. Otherwise, expand to an FQDN by appending the configured `domain` when the reference contains no dot and is not an IPv4 literal
3. Fetch the device list once and match, in priority order: exact case-insensitive name equality with the FQDN, then name starting with the short hostname followed by a dot, then case-insensitive substring containment of the short hostname
4. When no match is found, raise an error carrying the message `Device not found: <reference>`, which maps to a user-error exit code through the existing message-keyword rule

### 3.3 Schedule decoding

A private method taking a policy map and the shared `schedule` rules, producing
the report `schedule` object: `days` derived from `schedule_days` against
`day_bits`, `weeks_of_month` derived from `schedule_weeks_of_month` against
`week_bits`, `time` from `schedule_time`, `use_scheduled_timezone` from
`configuration`, and `next_remediation_reliable` set false whenever
`next_remediation` appears in `unreliable_fields`. When a bitmask decodes to an
empty set the raw integer is preserved in the report message rather than
silently dropped.

## 4. Report

### 4.1 New file: `groovy/src/main/groovy/ai/worklog/framework/automox/AutomoxOperatorReport.groovy`

Structural copy of `JenkinsOperatorReport` with these differences:

- Fields: `operation`, `fetchedAt`, `status`, `profile`, `org`, `message`, `query`, `group`, `device`, `policy`, `schedule`, `change`, `items`
- `fromPayload(Map)` following the same null-tolerant construction
- `toMap(Redaction)` emitting optional keys only when populated, matching the existing conditional-append style
- `renderJson(Redaction)` identical in shape, pretty-printed with a trailing line separator
- `renderHuman(Redaction)` with header `Automox <operation>` and indented lines for Profile, Org, Query, Group, Device, Policy, Fetched, Status, Message, then decoded schedule lines when present, then a change summary when present, then items
- `exitCodeFor(report, exitCodes)` reusing the Jenkins mapping: BLOCKED to blocked; ERROR to user error when the message contains `not found`, `invalid`, or `missing`, otherwise system error; anything else success
- `redactItem` preserving these boolean and identifier keys after redaction: `has_api_token`, `has_enrollment_key`, `connected`, `compliant`, `needs_reboot`, `applied`, `dry_run`, `installed`, `id`, `server_group_id`, `policy_id`

## 5. Command surface

### 5.1 New file: `groovy/src/main/groovy/ai/worklog/framework/commands/AutomoxCommands.groovy`

Structural copy of `JenkinsCommands` including its argument-parsing helpers,
error-report construction, and `rejectUnknownArgs` behaviour with the word
`automox` substituted in messages.

Usage line:
`Usage: ai-worklog automox {profiles|auth-test|orgs|groups|group|devices|device|policies|policy|policy-stats|device-move|policy-add-group} ...`

Global options consumed before dispatch: `--json`, `--profile <NAME>`,
`--apply`.

Dispatch table:

| Action | Positional | Options |
|---|---|---|
| `profiles` | none | none |
| `auth-test` | none | none |
| `orgs` | none | none |
| `groups` | none | `--query`, `--limit` |
| `group` | group id | none |
| `devices` | none | `--group`, `--query`, `--limit` |
| `device` | id or FQDN | `--packages` |
| `policies` | none | `--limit` |
| `policy` | policy id | none |
| `policy-stats` | none | `--policy` |
| `device-move` | id or FQDN, then group id | `--apply` |
| `policy-add-group` | policy id, then group id | `--apply` |

`--apply` must be rejected with `Unknown option for automox <action>: --apply`
on every action other than the two write actions.

Limit values are validated by the existing `parseLimit` helper semantics and
additionally clamped against the corresponding `limits.*_max` value from shared
rules, reporting `Invalid --limit: <value>` when above the maximum.

